## Why Do My Ombre Nails Look Streaky Instead of Blended?

Streaky ombre comes down to two habits: loading too much polish onto the sponge at once, and dragging it across the nail instead of dabbing. Dark fall shades make both mistakes more visible than a pastel would, because pigment concentration shows every uneven patch. Fix the loading and the motion, and the streak disappears, which is the entire technical foundation the rest of this <strong>fall ombre nails</strong> guide builds on.

Ombre is one colour fading into another on a single nail, cuticle to tip or the reverse. Gradient is the wider term, and it can mean a fade running across several colours or across the whole hand rather than confined to one nail, so if you're searching for inspiration and the two terms keep tangling, that is the actual distinction. The "swamp fingers" look, that murky, dotted-stippled texture some people end up with, happens when a heavily loaded sponge presses down and the colours mix on the nail rather than layering on top of each other. Burgundy and rust are unforgiving here. Their pigment load is heavier than a spring pastel, so a single overloaded dab reads as a solid smudge rather than a soft edge. The chalk dust on your fingers afterwards is a separate issue entirely, just dried polish transfer from a sponge that wasn't cleaned between colours, and it wipes away with the cuticle clean-up step later on.

