## Fall Ombre Nails with Gel Polish (Different Rules Apply)

Yes, you can do <strong>fall ombre nails</strong> with gel polish, but the sponge-only method doesn't translate directly. Gel needs curing between layers, and a brush or stipple technique gives more control than a sponge does, since you're working with a product that sets under a lamp rather than air-drying on its own timeline.

The most common gel ombre failure is curing too early. Uncured gel can still be adjusted, wiped back, or blended further, so the instinct to rush a layer under the lamp because it looks messy is exactly the wrong move. Leave it uncured until the blend genuinely looks right; gel doesn't set until the light hits it, so you have more working time than it feels like in the moment. Apply your base colour, cure, then add your second shade with a stipple brush rather than dragging it across in strokes, blending at the transition zone before you cure that layer. If it smears once cured, that's almost always a case of curing before the blend was finished, not a product failure. For the deeper year-round mechanics of both methods side by side, <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/blog/how-to-do-ombre-nails-at-home'>the full sponge and gel ombre technique breakdown for any season</a> is worth a read before your next attempt.

