## Three-Colour Fall Ombre: How to Avoid the Muddy Middle

A three-colour fade, rust into amber into gold, say, adds one extra failure point: the middle transition zone where two blends have to meet without collapsing into a third, unintended colour. The fix is keeping each handoff distinct before you blend it, rather than trying to merge all three at once.

Work outward from your anchor shade first, blending fully into your second colour and letting that transition settle before introducing the third. Trying to work all three zones simultaneously is what produces that flat, brownish middle nobody wanted, because the sponge keeps picking up residual pigment from the zone you just finished. A three-colour fade genuinely earns its complexity on longer nail shapes; on anything short, the individual bands get compressed to a point where they read as smudges rather than distinct tones. If your nail bed is on the shorter side, a two-colour combination will look more deliberate than a three-colour one squeezed into too little space.

